  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 325 AM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nVirtually no new updates since the previous forecast package. Any \nreports of light snow have remained in northern MN. Now that the \ntrough that had been driving this activity has moved east, a strong \n1035mb surface high will take hold through today and much of \nTuesday. Currently, blankets of low to mid level stratus continue to \nspan much of the state early this morning but subsidence will \nincrease from west to east and skies should become mostly clear by \nlate morning for MN and mid-afternoon for western WI. Winds will \nalso continue to decrease as this morning progresses. Today will not \nwarm up much as most locations observe upper 20s to near 30 for \nhighs temperatures. A few lucky spots near Chippewa Falls, Eau \nClaire, and Menomonie may even break the freezing mark. Temperatures \nbottom out in the low to mid teens tonight before another \"warm-up\" \nreturns for the second half of the week. \n\nOn late Tuesday into early Wednesday, amplified ridging over \nthe Pacific Northwest will influence the development of another \nwave over Alberta which then slides southeast down into northern\nplains. As this occurs, weak PVA looks to skirt portions of \nsouthwestern MN which could produce a short window of light snow\nWednesday morning. Ensemble guidance has approximately 30-40% \nof its membership advertising low QPF for this episode \ntherefore snow accumulations (if any) would be limited to only a\ntenth or two of an inch. This feature then continue southward \ninto the central plains where it converges on another developing\nlow near the ARKLATEX region by Thursday. This is when \nprecipition shield really amps up in converge but will remain \nwell off to our south and east yet could cause some travel \nimpacts right in time for the Thanksgiving holiday. As we enter \nnext weekend, another wave sweeps along the Canadian border \nwhich could serve as our next shot at some snow. But still too \nfar out to dive into any specifics just yet. As for temperatures\nWednesday through the remainder of the period, high return to \nthe upper 30s to lower 40s and lows in 20s and 30s.",
